WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.251
You fill out a form and leave a field blank.

00:00:05.251 --> 00:00:08.502
In a database that blank is called NULL, and it does not mean zero or empty.

00:00:08.502 --> 00:00:11.752
It means unknown.

00:00:11.752 --> 00:00:15.003
In normal logic, every test is TRUE or FALSE.

00:00:15.003 --> 00:00:18.254
Four point five greater than four, true; greater than five, false.

00:00:18.254 --> 00:00:21.505
But the moment NULL enters, any comparison with it is UNKNOWN, a third truth value.

00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:24.756
Four point five greater than NULL?

00:00:24.756 --> 00:00:28.007
Unknown.

00:00:28.007 --> 00:00:31.257
NULL equals NULL?

00:00:31.257 --> 00:00:34.508
Still unknown.

00:00:34.508 --> 00:00:37.759
And here is the bite: WHERE keeps only TRUE rows, so UNKNOWN, and every NULL, drops silently.

00:00:37.759 --> 00:00:41.010
Only is null can find them.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.267
Before NULL, a quick refresher on ordinary logic, where every condition is true or false.

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:47.525
AND needs both true: only true and true gives true.

00:00:47.525 --> 00:00:50.782
OR needs at least one: any true makes it true.

00:00:50.782 --> 00:00:54.040
And NOT just flips, true to false, false to true.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.297
Two values in, two values out.

00:00:57.297 --> 00:01:00.555
Predictable.

00:01:00.555 --> 00:01:03.812
NULL is about to break that.

00:01:03.812 --> 00:01:06.145
With NULL in play, the rules fall into two groups.

00:01:06.145 --> 00:01:08.478
First group: the operators.

00:01:08.478 --> 00:01:10.811
With AND, false wins.

00:01:10.811 --> 00:01:13.145
False and null is false.

00:01:13.145 --> 00:01:15.478
But true and null is null.

00:01:15.478 --> 00:01:17.811
With OR, true wins.

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:20.144
True or null is true.

00:01:20.144 --> 00:01:22.477
But false or null is null.

00:01:22.477 --> 00:01:24.810
And NOT just passes the unknown through: not null is null.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.144
So under operators, a known value can still decide the answer.

00:01:27.144 --> 00:01:29.477
Second group: comparisons.

00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:31.810
Here nothing decides.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.143
Four point five greater than null?

00:01:34.143 --> 00:01:36.476
Null.

00:01:36.476 --> 00:01:38.809
Four equals null?

00:01:38.809 --> 00:01:41.143
Null.

00:01:41.143 --> 00:01:43.476
Null equals null itself?

00:01:43.476 --> 00:01:45.809
Still null.

00:01:45.809 --> 00:01:48.142
Every comparison with null is unknown.

00:01:48.142 --> 00:01:50.475
The one escape is is null.

00:01:50.475 --> 00:01:52.808
Is this value null?

00:01:52.808 --> 00:01:55.142
That returns a real true or false.

00:01:55.142 --> 00:01:57.475
It is the only test that finds a null.

00:01:57.475 --> 00:01:59.808
Five patterns to remember.

00:01:59.808 --> 00:02:02.141
False wins, true wins, null stays, null wins, is null tests.

00:02:02.141 --> 00:02:05.406
Watch one row decide its own fate.

00:02:05.406 --> 00:02:08.670
Focus on Daffy, row nine.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.935
Daffy's user-ID is three, so user-ID equals three is true.

00:02:11.935 --> 00:02:15.200
But Daffy's rating is null, so rating greater than four is unknown.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.465
Now the operator decides.

00:02:18.465 --> 00:02:21.729
Under AND, true and unknown is unknown, and WHERE drops the row.

00:02:21.729 --> 00:02:24.994
Daffy is gone.

00:02:24.994 --> 00:02:28.259
But under OR, true or unknown is true, and WHERE keeps the row.

00:02:28.259 --> 00:02:31.523
Daffy stays.

00:02:31.523 --> 00:02:34.788
Same row, two operators, two fates.

00:02:34.788 --> 00:02:38.116
So how do you find NULL rows, and why do they vanish?

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:41.444
Three filters, side by side.

00:02:41.444 --> 00:02:44.773
Query one: WHERE rating is null.

00:02:44.773 --> 00:02:48.101
It returns row nine, Daffy's NULL listen.

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:51.429
Is null, the test, gives a definite TRUE, so WHERE keeps it.

00:02:51.429 --> 00:02:54.757
Query two: WHERE rating greater than four.

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:58.085
Five rows, but row nine is gone.

00:02:58.085 --> 00:03:01.413
Query three: WHERE rating less than or equal to four.

00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:04.742
Three rows, and row nine is gone again.

00:03:04.742 --> 00:03:08.070
Every comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN, and WHERE keeps only TRUE rows.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.398
So the NULL silently disappears from both comparisons.

00:03:11.398 --> 00:03:14.726
Only is null finds it.

00:03:14.726 --> 00:03:17.447
NULL does not just change comparisons.

00:03:17.447 --> 00:03:20.169
It changes math.

00:03:20.169 --> 00:03:22.890
Here is the rating column.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.612
Nine rows, and one of them is null.

00:03:25.612 --> 00:03:28.333
First, count star.

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:31.054
It counts every row, including the null.

00:03:31.054 --> 00:03:33.776
So count star is nine.

00:03:33.776 --> 00:03:36.497
Now count of rating.

00:03:36.497 --> 00:03:39.219
It skips the null row.

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:41.940
So count of rating is eight, not nine.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.661
And average.

00:03:44.661 --> 00:03:47.383
It sums the eight known ratings, thirty three point six, and divides by eight, not nine.

00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:50.104
So the average is four point two.

00:03:50.104 --> 00:03:52.826
Sum, min, and max all skip the null the same way.

00:03:52.826 --> 00:03:55.547
If you forget this, your averages quietly divide by a smaller number than you think.

00:03:55.547 --> 00:03:59.906
Four lines to remember.

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:04.264
NULL means unknown, not zero.

00:04:04.264 --> 00:04:08.623
Any operation involving NULL returns NULL.

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:12.982
WHERE drops NULL rows silently.

00:04:12.982 --> 00:04:17.341
And is null is the only way to test for it directly.

00:04:17.341 --> 00:04:21.699
Look at the trap on the slide: WHERE rating equals NULL is the wrong way, WHERE rating is null is the right way.

00:04:21.699 --> 00:04:26.058
Internalize those four lines and you have defeated half the SQL bugs that exist in production.
